{
  "species": "lucanus-cervus",
  "constant": "LUCANUS_CERVUS_PLATE",
  "order": "coleoptera",
  "sex": "male",
  "hallmark": "large antler-like mandibles",
  "reference": {
    "title": "Käferbuch. Naturgeschichte der Käfer Europas, table 22 figure 20",
    "artist": "Emil Hochdanz",
    "year": 1876,
    "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucanus.cervus.male.-.calwer.22.20.jpg",
    "licence": "Public domain (PD-old-100-1923)"
  },
  "doc": [
    "*Lucanus cervus*, male, dorsal — the first hand-authored plate.",
    "",
    "Traced from Emil Hochdanz's lithograph in Calwer's *Käferbuch* (1876), which",
    "is public domain; `references/SOURCES.md` has the provenance and",
    "`references/lucanus-cervus.jpg` is the file. What was taken from the",
    "reference is the silhouette, the proportions and the arrangement of the",
    "limbs, measured off the image row by row rather than eyeballed. What was left",
    "behind is everything that makes a lithograph a lithograph: the stipple, the",
    "highlights, the colour. This is an engraving in three line weights.",
    "",
    "## Reading the coordinates",
    "",
    "Plate space: the midline is x = 0 and y runs 0 at the mandible tips to 1000",
    "at the elytral apex. The tarsi reach past 1200 and the fore legs past x = 300,",
    "which is correct — a pinned male stag beetle is much wider and longer than",
    "its body, and the renderer measures the drawing rather than assuming a frame.",
    "",
    "## What is authored once and what is authored whole",
    "",
    "Almost everything is the **right half**, reflected by the renderer: mandible,",
    "eye, antenna, palp, elytron, all nine leg segments, every stroke of hatching.",
    "Six parts declare `mirror: false` because they straddle the axis and are",
    "symmetric in themselves — head, pronotum and its two grooves, scutellum, and",
    "the basal band. Reflecting those would draw each of them twice, one copy a",
    "fraction off the other, and at plate size that shows as a doubled line.",
    "",
    "Those six were still authored from a right-hand profile and reflected *here*,",
    "at authoring time, rather than typed out twice: `validatePlate` checks that",
    "each is centred on the axis, and each is exact.",
    "",
    "## Limbs",
    "",
    "Each femur and tibia is a **capsule**: the outline runs up one side, round",
    "the far end, back down the other and round again, so the nth anchor and its",
    "partner from the other end are the two edges of one cross-section. Widths are",
    "measured off the lithograph against the width across the wing cases — a femur",
    "is about six per cent of it and a tibia about four, which at 300 units across",
    "the elytra is 18 and 11.5. The first pass drew them half again as heavy and",
    "the animal looked moulded rather than engraved.",
    "",
    "Every tibia carries four spines on its outer margin, raked towards the foot.",
    "They are detail rank, so they leave the drawing at thumbnail size along with",
    "the hatching and the striae.",
    "",
    "## Drawing order",
    "",
    "Back to front, because the array is the stacking and the renderer does no",
    "sorting. Legs first, so the femora disappear under the body where they",
    "should; then the elytra and their surface work; then the thorax, the head,",
    "and the mandibles last, over everything."
  ],
